WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
Authorization is not paperwork that sits outside offensive-security skill.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.720
It is part of the skill.

3
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:15.180
A capable researcher knows that testing only becomes legitimate when the activity is permitted, scoped, and controlled.

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.800
A public system, a visible endpoint, or a helpful intention does not create permission.

5
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.580
Public proof should therefore show how the work stayed inside boundaries, not just what was found.

6
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:29.020
Good scoping starts before testing.

7
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:41.920
Read the policy, program page, contract, lab rules, or written authorization that defines allowed assets, techniques, data handling, reporting channels, and stop conditions.

8
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:45.140
If a boundary is unclear, ask before acting.

9
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:56.120
Some programs allow safe validation but prohibit persistence, social engineering, denial of service, destructive testing, or access to other people's data.

10
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:04.140
A portfolio artifact can mention that testing was performed in an authorized environment without exposing private program terms.

11
00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:08.980
Ethical judgment also includes minimizing contact with sensitive data.

12
00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:17.720
If a result can be proven with a harmless marker, a synthetic record, or a screenshot that redacts identifying details, use that approach.

13
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.500
Do not collect more data than needed to demonstrate risk.

14
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:25.360
Do not continue exploring after the vulnerability is established.

15
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:34.100
Do not turn a validation step into an investigation of users, customers, employees, or unrelated systems.

16
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.240
The restraint is part of the evidence.

17
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:41.040
Legal context matters, but this course is not legal advice.

18
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:53.540
Policies from government agencies, coordinated disclosure groups, and platform operators can help researchers understand common expectations, yet they do not turn unauthorized activity into authorized work.

19
00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:05.720
The practical lesson is simple: preserve written authorization, follow the stated scope, document decisions, report promptly, and avoid claiming a legal shield you do not personally have.

20
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.520
Researcher judgment becomes visible in the final artifact.

21
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:17.700
A strong report explains what was allowed, what was tested, what was intentionally avoided, and what uncertainty remains.

22
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.320
A hiring manager, program triager, or peer reviewer should see more than technical curiosity.

23
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.380
They should see that you can operate safely when the stakes are real.

24
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.880
Offensive-security credibility is built when skill and restraint appear together.

25
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.840
For a public portfolio, make this restraint easy to notice.

26
00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:42.380
Include a short authorization note, scope summary, evidence-handling statement, and limitation statement when the artifact calls for them.

27
00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.120
Those details do not need to be long, and they should not expose private terms.

28
00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:52.740
They simply show that your technical decisions were made inside a responsible operating model.
